{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789846553,"nonce":"N0NvuNmzMMxcNAPgFwt9sJPKo70=","message":"Sticky-mark minor collections landed in 982034a, ON BY DEFAULT on unix (ASH_GC_GEN=0 turns them off; ASH_GC_MAJOR_EVERY, default 8).\n\nHow it works: the generational sweep leaves the marks of every block it keeps; a major clears them all first (clear_all_marks). A minor skips protected-and-clean blocks entirely (not traced, not swept), pushes every marked object in the other blocks with sticky marks (dirty old blocks, TLAB blocks; a young block has no marks so it costs nothing) as roots via old_roots(), traces from there and from the ordinary roots -- mark_allocation_shared already stops at a marked object, which is the whole trick -- then sweeps only the non-clean blocks. Kept blocks are protected again, including sparse ones: a TLAB refill that takes a recycled span faults once and that is what marks the block for scanning; leaving sparse blocks writable meant rescanning every sparse block's old objects at every minor (that was the first version, and on a synthetic with 100k-entry arrays rewritten each round it cost more than a major). A spanning object across several dirty blocks is pushed once. The next collection is a major when the kept set exceeds twice what the last major kept plus 64 blocks, which bounds floating garbage.\n\nNumbers, NUC: binary_trees minors mark in 0.25-1.3ms where a major marks in 5-6ms; pause total 37.5 -\u003e 16.2ms, max 8.3 -\u003e 4.4ms; wall -9.8% in one clean sweep (357 vs 396ms hybrid-auto), -3.5%/-6.8% in a later sweep taken while the box was in interactive use. Adversarial synthetic (20k old nodes rewritten to young children every round, 3M churn): pause total 105 -\u003e 48..80ms, max pause unchanged, live set 5.7 -\u003e 9..14MB (floating garbage until the major). macOS: SIGBUS path works, binary_trees pause total 39 -\u003e 21ms, and the old-to-young test passes with 490 dirtied blocks counted.\n\nGates: conformance 1372/1372 on interp/hybrid/jit/aot with the default; a minor-only stress pass (ASH_GC_STRESS=64, ASH_GC_MAJOR_EVERY=1000) 1372/1372 on unit+threads; parity 3 modes, aot_smoke, osr_retier green; std unit tests. Corpus A/B vs ASH_GC_GEN=0: nothing outside the noise band besides binary_trees.\n\nNot measured: a game. MBHaxe's per-frame writes into a large old entity set are the pattern where a minor scans the most (every dirty block whole, at block granularity); that costs at most what a major cost before, plus one fault per written old block per cycle.","files":null}]}